@startuml contract-test
' Why the contract test has two layers, and what each one pins to what.
' Grounded in tests/contract_frame_test.c, tests/frame_gate.c,
' Src/telemetry.c, and SerialFrameParserTest.cpp in industrial-hmi.

title Contract test: pinning the frames to the host's parser rules

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam componentStyle rectangle

package "hmi-edge-node (this repo)" {
  [telemetry\n(pure logic)]        as Tel
  [fake reader +\ncapturing sink]  as Doubles
  [frame_gate\n(the host's rules,\ntranscribed)] as Gate
  [contract_frame_test]            as Test
}

package "industrial-hmi (separate repo, no code shared)" {
  [SerialFrameParserTest.cpp] as HostTest
  [SerialFrameParser]         as HostParser
}

Test --> Doubles  : injects
Doubles --> Tel   : drives telemetry_update()
Tel --> Test      : the exact bytes\n("equipment/0/state,on\\n"\n "temp,23.5\\n")

Test --> Gate     : layer 2\nfeed the firmware's bytes
HostTest ..> Gate : layer 1\nsamples copied verbatim\n("temp,23.5", "a,b,c", ",23", ...)

HostTest --> HostParser : tests
HostParser ..> Tel      : reads on the wire\n(115200 8N1, ADR-0029)

note bottom of Gate
  Layer 1 fails FIRST if the transcribed
  rules drift from the host's. Only then
  does layer 2 say anything about firmware.
end note

note right of Test
  Two layers on purpose. With literal
  assertions alone, a format change could
  be "fixed" on both sides at once and the
  contract would break in silence. The gate
  is anchored to the host's own samples, so
  it cannot be edited into agreement.
end note

note bottom of HostParser
  No build dependency between the repos.
  They share a wire protocol, not code.
end note

@enduml
